WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:03.840
Hey Wisecrack.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:04.850
Jared here.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.340
Today we have a special episode on an artist
who forces us to think about our own mortality,

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.560
among other things-

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.400
"What's the point of thinking?"

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.780
Kanye West.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.080
Following Kanye gives you the feeling of watching
a train filled with $300 sneakers crash into

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.910
a guy dressed as Jesus.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.310
But at the same time, he projects a certain
sincerity that seems to contradict his otherwise

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.440
impulsive image.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.440
"I'm really happy for you and I'ma let you finish..."

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:34.840
There's no doubt that Kanye thinks Kanye's a
philosopher.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:40.320
I don’t think we should go quite that far, but Kanye does share at least one concern with 19th century

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.260
Danish philosopher Søren Kierkegaard: death, Like, a lot of feelings about death.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.080
Welcome to this Wisecrack edition on the Philosophy of Kanye West and Death.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.960
Kanye has never been shy about mentioning
death.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.260
"We're all gonna die one day."

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.640
In his 2005 Grammy Award speech, he spoke
about his experience with a car accident

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.680
and his discovery that:

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.820
"Nothing in life is promised except death."

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.820
Kanye has repeated similar sentiments throughout
his career.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.580
"Only luxury is time. Only thing promised is that you gonna die one day.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.720
"So we just gonna live it to the maximum."

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.979
“Through the Wire,” recorded when Ye’s
jaw was still wired shut from his 2002 car

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:21.759
accident, describes his narrow miss with death
and his “triumph” over the situation at

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:24.600
the hands of a guardian angel.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.480
Frankly, I’m surprised he gave the angel
any credit at all.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.380
This interest in death is pretty explicit
starting from his first album, The College

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.820
Dropout, where we hear:

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.160
On one of his most recent tracks, Waves, he keeps it short and simple:

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.000
Waves don't die.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.859
In "Power" Kanye

00:01:53.859 --> 00:02:01.200
fantasizes about a "beautiful death" And his
30 minute art film Runaway opens with an excerpt

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.569
from Mozart's Requiem- which,
is, of course, a mass for the dead.

00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:07.299
Okay, so Kanye has some strong feelings about
death.

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:08.299
So what?

00:02:08.299 --> 00:02:12.550
Kierkegaard’s philosophy tells us that despair
over our mortality is pretty inevitable unless

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.850
we embrace our relationship with the big guy
upstairs.- the notorious G.O.D.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.350
Jesus is pretty key to coping with our worries
about death because having faith in him means

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.860
that death isn’t actually the end for you.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.930
For Kanye, this despiar is revealed in his
fixation on mortality, his desire to live in the moment, and perhaps, his desire

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.770
to evade death altogether.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.020
You know the saying, that a man may die but
an idea will live forever?

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.420
Kanye has bought into that big-time, except
the idea that will preserve Kanye is…

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.020
Kanye.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.140
"You don't think I'd be one of the characters of today's modern bible?"

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.200
By positioning himself as musician,
producer, fashion designer, reality star, and

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.820
frequent commentator on… everything and
whatever he next pursues.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.500
"I have decided, in 2020, to run for president."

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.180
Kanye is doing more than just expressing himself.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.860
Maybe he's trying to cultivate a kind of celebrity
that is so ubiquitous, that he achieves some

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.320
kind of immortality.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.800
Like Kierkegaard’s works, Kanye deals with
mortality in three ways: distracting himself

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.910
with cool sh*t, trying to be a good person,
and striving to somehow make himself eternal

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.380
so he doesn’t have to worry about all that
sh*t anyway.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.130
Or in the words of Kierkegaard, the aesthetic,
the ethical, and the religious.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:24.340
Kanye is a walking contradiction: private
and radically public, meticulous and spontaneous,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.040
"Beyoncé had one of the best videos of all time!"

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.500
So why can’t he live the contradiction of embracing
his inevitable death and also trying to

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.630
live forever? Bear with me for a second.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:38.330
Kierkegaard tells us that in order to be cured
of the despair that arises from this contradiction-

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:42.790
one must understand his or her own self and
the nature of that self, and to get there,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.490
they can progress through three consecutive
stages of existence.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:47.319
Kanye does all three.

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:52.370
Is the similarity to Kierkegaard a total accident
born of too much cocaine and a god-complex

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.470
bigger than Kim Kardashian’s, uh, personality?

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.900
Possibly, but we’re here to figure it out
anyway.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.420
So let's get to it.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:05.690
The hedonistic spectacle of Kanye is pretty
much exactly what Kierkegaard would define

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:06.690
as the “aesthetic.”

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:10.209
We can see this in Kierkegaard’s 1843 philosophy
text.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:13.670
Either/Or: “grab a drink, grab a glass,
after that I grab yo ass.”

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.360
Kidding; that’s definitely Kanye.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.729
But Kierkegaard does tell us that the aesthetic
man lives his life in the pursuit of desire

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:23.259
and pleasure, often interpreting himself as
though he were always on stage.

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:24.460
Sound familiar?

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.500
To get a better grasp of Kierkegaard’s stages,
think about a corndog.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.150
When you’re young, corn dogs are really
delicious, and that’s pretty much all that

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.150
matters.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.100
You don’t think about the stomach ache you’ll
have in the morning or about the future of

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.009
your bowel movements. Corn dog= yum.

00:04:39.009 --> 00:04:40.680
That’s the aesthetic stage.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.939
It’s easy to find examples of aesthetic
debauchery in Kanye’s work although that’s

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:46.680
not exactly unique among rappers.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.719
What’s more interesting is the way in which
Kanye’s aesthetic embodiment is so over

00:04:50.719 --> 00:04:52.139
the top and enduring.

00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:56.680
As seen most vividly in his music videos,
Kanye takes the typical sex and drugs imagery

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:01.680
of a hip hop video and draws out the obscenity,
makes it self-aware, and really crafts it

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.159
into a kind of art.

00:05:03.159 --> 00:05:07.219
This is the video for “Famous,” off his
most recent album, and while it doesn’t

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:11.680
shy away from controversy, it’s also a pretty
interesting social and political statement.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.800
"I'm motha f*ckin Kanye West."

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.680
But for someone who lives on a stage, both
metaphorically and also kind of literally,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.199
Kanye has a lot to say about his privacy.

00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:25.270
Kanye has said that one of his biggest concerns
with paparazzi is the effect that they have

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.750
on his family, especially his daughter.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:31.480
This is a different side of Kanye than the
one we see yelling about being Shakespeare,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.660
"I am Shakespeare, in the flesh."

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.540
but it’s certainly not an altogether different
character.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:41.080
Far from it, Kanye’s sometimes-outlandish
aesthetic serves as a kind of frame to his

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.350
familial and ethical considerations.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:46.930
And we know Kanye loves frames.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:54.120
Kanye’s relationship with his wife and children
is one of his most clearly humanizing characteristics.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:58.500
"Family time. It's what Kim gave me. She gave me everything."

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.740
Using one of his many pseudonymous characters,
Kierkegaard argues that the transition from

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:07.550
the aesthetic stage of self-discovery to the
ethical stage is exemplified in marriage.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:12.180
A person in the aesthetic stage is focused
only on the momentary pleasures of the erotic,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.839
and marriage forces them to commit to something
long-term, which changes their priorities.

00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:21.169
We see evidence of this in Kanye’s most
recent album, The Life of Pablo, the first

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:22.870
he has released since marrying Kim.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:27.259
Pitchfork describes this album as “the moment…
that the artist finally settles down” after

00:06:27.259 --> 00:06:31.280
years of turbulent affairs and, uh, probably
one too many pregnancy scares.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.629
Let’s go back to our corndog —at some
point, you might discover that corn production

00:06:35.629 --> 00:06:39.610
requires a tremendous amount of water and
has a lot of potential to do economic and

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:40.780
environmental damage.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.939
Then, even your own personal health concerns
might take a backseat to your commitment to

00:06:44.939 --> 00:06:47.660
something higher than yourself: the welfare
of the planet.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.639
That’s Kierkegaard’s ethical stage.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:54.000
Besides his relationship with his family,
we see Kanye most actively engaged in the

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.980
ethical sphere in his advocacy for racial
and class justice.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.180
"I care about people!"

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:04.840
Kanye has been vocal in addressing inequalities
since the very beginning of his career,

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:09.680
"Everybody in hip hop discriminates against gay people."

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.260
this hasn’t changed as he’s gathered more
fame and different projects.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.920
"George Bush doesn't care about black people."

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:20.380
And he draws much of his musical influences
from soul music of the civil rights era.

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:24.740
We also see consistent mentions of racial
and class justice in his own lyrics.

00:07:24.749 --> 00:07:29.369
The message that we hear on “Crack Music”
from 2005 is a similar one to the messages

00:07:29.369 --> 00:07:33.879
in “Murder to Excellence” in 2011 and
“New Slaves” in 2013.

00:07:33.879 --> 00:07:38.059
Kanye's work often speaks to the ways in which
deeply- entrenched power dynamics within our

00:07:38.059 --> 00:07:42.919
society can perpetuate the oppression of people
of color and the working class.

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:47.449
His commitment to certain moral ideas about
justice outside of just what benefits him

00:07:47.449 --> 00:07:52.289
or his family is the mark of someone operating
in the ethical stage of self-discovery.

00:07:52.289 --> 00:07:54.380
Or just, you know, common human decency.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:59.820
"Half the time, sixty, seventy percent, I'm not saying the popular opinion. I'm saying something I believe."

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:06.039
Remember that Kierkegaard says that the whole
reason to even talk about these stages is

00:08:06.039 --> 00:08:08.469
because they help you to escape despair.

00:08:08.469 --> 00:08:12.899
To escape despair, you must accept your true
nature (which means realizing that you are

00:08:12.899 --> 00:08:15.780
bound to Christ) and then you can basically
just chill the fuck out.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.689
Kierkegaard is 100% on board the Jesus boat,
so he’s not actually worried about his own

00:08:20.689 --> 00:08:24.939
death, but he knows you probably are, and
he just wants to throw you a big ol’ Christian

00:08:24.939 --> 00:08:29.550
lifesaver. Kanye has one leg in the Jesus boat,
and one leg in his own boat.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.900
On one hand, he has been consistently vocal
about his relationship to the big J.C. Remember

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.039
that one of his first big hits was “Jesus
Walks,” and that theme of religious devotion

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:50.660
has stayed in his music even up through his latest album, which samples Gospel tracks and opens with the lyrics:

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:56.120
But on the other hand, Kanye is pretty insistent
that he is God and he doesn’t seem to

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:58.100
be too concerned about blasphemy.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:01.620
We could really spend an entire day dissecting
just that one song.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:06.090
He calls himself a god, he acknowledges his
connection to another god and he is extremely

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:09.510
insistent on the timely delivery of his baked
goods.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:14.360
That’s not the only time Kanye has referred
to himself as a God.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.630
I mean, the guy named himself “Yeezus.”

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:21.200
In “See Me Now”, a bonus track off My
Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy, he says he

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.750
is Lord and refers to his GDs, which Rap Genius
tells me means “gangsta disciples.”

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:31.260
He’s also spoken about his apparent divinity
on stage and in interviews, where he gives

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:33.360
a somewhat more nuanced approach:

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.820
"I just told you who I thought I was: a god."

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.780
Wow, I really can't argue with that. Alright Kanye you win...you're a God.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.480
"Jesus wept... I know"

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.520
Angelic language and imagery appear all over his work.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.080
The presence of angels is especially strong
in his music videos: “No Church in the Wild,”

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.510
“Power,” and the 30-minute film “Runaway”
all feature winged women.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:56.150
He also just released a trailer for a video
game which focuses on his dead mother’s

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.170
ascension to heaven.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:57.670
No, really.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:58.160
That’s a thing.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.440
The game itself hasn’t been released yet, but we’re guessing that the player gets to guide Mama West

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:07.400
up to the pearly gates, which is basically leaving the fate of his mother’s eternal salvation in the hands of gamers.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.560
Not sure Kanye thought that one through.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:15.060
Kanye’s tense relationship between himself
as God and the Christian God might be representative

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:18.250
of the despair that he is trying to evade.

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:24.170
As previously mentioned, despair is characterized
by a tension between a desire for the temporal—the

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.580
right-now-on-Earth—and the eternal.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:30.060
As a God, Kanye hopes to achieve earthly immortality
through the legacy of his work, while as a

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.870
believer in another God, Kanye gives over
his life to someone else.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:37.610
For Kierkegaard, religion is the highest stage
of existence and self-discovery.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:42.380
It’s where you realize that the corndog
actually belongs to someone else, and oh damn,

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:43.850
where did this corndog come from?

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:45.660
How did it even end up in my hand?

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:46.660
Where am I?

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:47.660
Who are you?

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:48.660
What are you doing with all those corndogs?

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:52.160
Yeah, now you’re the corndog, and you belong
to Jesus.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:56.810
In the religious stage, you recognize that
reason and belief can only get you so far

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:01.490
Rather than belief, which can be grounded
in proof or in reason, you need faith, which

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.830
is totally beyond the bounds of logic.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:08.350
Kierkegaard describes faith as a “passionate
inwardness,” which is also probably a pretty

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:10.140
good description for Kanye.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:14.390
That “beyond logic” part is important
since coming up with rational arguments about

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:17.840
death has never made anyone feel more okay
about death.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.270
When you look at it scientifically, rotting
in a hole isn’t all that great.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:25.450
I suppose one way to no longer care about
death is to believe in your own godliness.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.640
The immortality of his work isn’t important
at all unless he knows it, so the performance

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.410
art trainwreck that is Kanye West’s public
persona really just exists as a conversation

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:35.640
with himself.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:36.600
But does that surprise you?

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:40.740
"I will die for the art, for what I believe in."

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:46.920
So Kanye is trying to turn himself into a
God at the same time that he believes in the

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:51.290
God of Christianity, and he’s doing this
because he’s really confused about death.

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:55.740
But how much of his aesthetic, ethical, and
religious action is just a matter of him saying

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:58.750
whatever pops into his head and how much is
intentional?

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:02.330
It’s impossible to say for sure, but maybe
it’s worth some thought.

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:05.940
He’s a lot smarter than people are sometimes
willing to give him credit for.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:10.980
Can Kanye really escape his anxieties about death by creating an artistic legacy to leave behind?

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:16.600
Forget the sick beats or the VMA antics—maybe
the real reason Kanye has such a massive following;

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:20.000
he gives voice to a certain dread that we
all have in common.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.600
Or maybe he’s just really good at Twitter.

